Ah, gotcha. I'm not opposed to that but I think it would be best handled as a separate commit/PR (Despite the numerous tools, it seems that multiple commits in a single PR is quite difficult to review). WDYT?

I guess alternatively, because this is all happening manually we could do something like:
```go
type CreatedByMeta interface {
    isCreatedByMeta()
}

type CreatedByScheduledJob struct {
    ScheduleID ScheduleID
}

type CreatedByUnknown struct {
    Name string
    ID string
}
```

Which would force users of the field to perform a type switch and mitigate most accidental misusage. It's certainly a bit more heavy handed that way though.

@dt, so I poked around the code a little bit and I think that adding a second field is likely going to cause more problems than it would solve. It doesn't seem particularly ergonomic and there's a codepath in the schematelemetry job that could make a scheduled job or a manually spawned job.

We can probably get the same benefits you're thinking of by adding a method to the createdbyinfo struct and optionally a constructor.

```go

// Or we can make this an int64 for now and merge it ahead of this commit.
func (i *CreatedByInfo) ScheduleID() ScheduleID {
    if i.Name != CreatedBySchedule {
        return 0 // Or the InvalidScheduleID constant    
    }
    return ScheduleID(i.ID)
}

// Optionally
func (i *CreatedByInfo) CreatedBySchedule() bool {
    return i.Name != CreatedBySchedule
}

// Optionally
func CreatedBySchedule(scheduleID ScheduleID) *CreatedByInfo {
    // You get the idea
}
```

IMO, this gives us the type safety without the overhead of having to stumble across multiple fields during construction, marshalling, and unmarshalling. Misusage of the method would carry the same risks as misuse of the fields. If we ever want to "first class" other creator types we can tack on similar methods and/or constructors. WDYT?
